;;;;
;;;; Risks and Technical Debt of the SBOM Tool (arc42 section 11)
;;;;
#{{:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/duplicated-bounded-pmap
   :name "bounded-pmap duplicated in three adapters"
   :desc "The same fixed-thread-pool helper is copied into the osv, deps-dev and github-advisory adapters, so a fix must be applied three times."
   :doc "### Description

adapter.vulnerability.osv, deps-dev and github-advisory each define a private bounded-pmap. The plans chose to duplicate it locally rather than share it (dev/plans/osv-vulnerability-adapter.md). A shared helper would need a home that adapters may depend on without breaking the layering, e.g. a small utility namespace.

### Impact

Divergent fixes and behavior between adapters, and each future adapter (e.g. NVD) adds a fourth copy.

### Mitigation

Extract one shared helper. Not started."
   :tags #{"technical debt"}}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/duplicated-warn-helpers
   :name "warning helper duplicated per adapter"
   :desc "Each vulnerability and VEX adapter defines its own private warn function that prints 'Warning: ...' to stderr."
   :doc "### Description

adapter.vex.openvex and the osv, deps-dev and github-advisory adapters each define a private warn, and the SBOM adapters print their warnings inline.

### Impact

The warning format and the destination stream can drift. Changing diagnostics (e.g. adding a verbosity switch) means touching every adapter.

### Mitigation

Introduce one shared diagnostics function, together with the bounded-pmap extraction."
   :tags #{"technical debt"}}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/spdx-license-list-underused
   :name "SPDX license list not used as the source of truth"
   :desc "Identifiability is decided by a LicenseRef- prefix heuristic, and the list's deprecated flag is read but not reported."
   :doc "### Description

domain.license/spdx-identifiable? treats every id without a LicenseRef- prefix as resolvable, so an unknown or misspelled id counts as identified. adapter.license.spdx reads :deprecated? for every license, but no report uses it. The list is a local file or a bundled snapshot, never refreshed at runtime.

### Impact

Wrong or deprecated license ids are not flagged distinctly, so license reports may overstate how many components are identified.

### Mitigation

Open ideas in dev/ideas.md (SPDX Licenses)."
   :tags #{"technical debt"}}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/failed-lookup-looks-like-clean-result
   :name "failed vulnerability lookup is indistinguishable from no findings"
   :desc "A failed live lookup only warns on stderr and yields no vulnerabilities, so --fail-on-violations can pass on incomplete data."
   :doc "### Description

Live lookups are fail-soft by decision (opt-in-fail-soft-live-lookup, failure-policy-by-input-kind). The report and the exit status carry no trace of a degraded lookup.

### Impact

A CI gate can report success although a vulnerability source was unreachable. This matters most for the goal ci-gate.

### Mitigation

Open idea in dev/ideas.md ('Vulnerability Database Adapters'): treat lookup errors differently from an empty result, e.g. in the report and as a distinct or opt-in strict exit code (a public contract, so tier 3)."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/unverified-external-api-assumptions
   :name "assumptions about external APIs are unverified"
   :desc "The OSV API-key header and the GitHub 'affects' version filtering rest on guesses recorded in the plans."
   :doc "### Description

adapter.vulnerability.osv/auth-headers sends X-Goog-Api-Key, which OSV does not document (the public API needs no key). The GitHub adapter assumes that the affects=package@version filter performs server-side version-range filtering, which the plan calls undocumented and unverified against a live call.

### Impact

If an assumption is wrong, a key is ignored or rejected, or results contain false positives or negatives without any error.

### Mitigation

Confirm against the services' documentation or a live call and record the outcome in the plans. The status of this check is unknown."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/network-paths-untested
   :name "real network calls are not covered by the automated tests"
   :desc "The vulnerability adapters' HTTP calls are deliberately not exercised by bb test, so API changes surface only in real use."
   :doc "### Description

The plans keep the real query and fetch calls out of the test suite to keep tests offline and deterministic. Mapping and chunking logic is covered, the transport is not.

### Impact

Changes in the OSV, deps.dev or GitHub responses or limits are noticed late.

### Mitigation

An opt-in integration test or a periodic manual check. Not defined."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/external-service-limits
   :name "rate limits and network environments of external services"
   :desc "Rate limits, throttling and restricted networks can slow or break live lookups on large SBOMs or in CI."
   :doc "### Description

deps.dev has no batch endpoint and no documented hard limit, so the concurrency default is a guess. GitHub's unauthenticated budget is shared with anything else on the same IP or token. Restrictive networks can fail on the JVM trust store. The proposed NVD adapter would take about three minutes for 30 CPEs without a key.

### Impact

Slow runs, failed chunks that are silently dropped (see failed-lookup-looks-like-clean-result), and flaky CI.

### Mitigation

Bounded concurrency and batching (decision bounded-concurrent-lookups), optional API keys, --debug for diagnosis. No cross-run cache (non-goal no-cross-run-caching)."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/vex-conflict-precedence-coarse
   :name "VEX conflict resolution may be too coarse"
   :desc "The most conservative status always wins, so a supplier's later, corrected not-affected statement can never override an older affected one."
   :doc "### Description

Decision vex-most-conservative-status-wins was chosen for safety and simplicity, and the OpenVEX specification mandates no resolution strategy.

### Impact

False positives that cannot be suppressed when several suppliers' documents conflict.

### Mitigation

Revisit if real multi-supplier conflicts occur, e.g. by weighing statement timestamps. Changing it is tier 3."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/cvss-v4-not-scored
   :name "CVSS v4 vectors are not scored"
   :desc "Only CVSS v2 and v3 are parsed, so findings that carry only a v4 vector rate as :unknown."
   :doc "### Description

domain.cvss supports v2 and v3. The v4 parser is only a proposal (dev/plans/cvss-v4-vector-parser.md).

### Impact

Vulnerabilities rated only by a v4 vector can fall under unknown severity in reports and policy decisions.

### Mitigation

Implement the proposed v4 parser."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/unsanitized-markdown-output
   :name "untrusted text reaches markdown reports unsanitized"
   :desc "Component names, license names/URLs and advisory text from SBOMs and APIs are written into markdown with only | and newlines escaped."
   :doc "### Description

adapter.report.markdown/md-escape escapes only the pipe and newlines. format-license-name builds a link from the license URL, which adapter.sbom.cdx takes unchecked from the SBOM. Advisory summaries and ids from the external services are rendered as well. Found in the security assessment at commit 5279795; confidence high.

### Impact

A crafted SBOM can inject links (any URL scheme) or break the report structure. The effect depends on where the markdown is rendered, e.g. a CI summary, a wiki or a PR comment.

### Mitigation

Escape markdown metacharacters in labels and allow only http and https URLs (see requirement safe-report-output). Not started."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/no-network-timeouts-or-size-limits
   :name "HTTP calls have no timeouts and no response size limit"
   :desc "The vulnerability adapters use a default HttpClient without connect or request timeout and read whole response bodies, so a stalled endpoint hangs the run."
   :doc "### Description

osv, deps-dev and github-advisory create HttpClient/newHttpClient and send requests without a timeout. Bodies are read fully as strings, and the futures in bounded-pmap are awaited without a timeout. Found in the security assessment at commit 5279795 (osv only skimmed); confidence high for github-advisory and deps-dev.

### Impact

A stalled, slow or hostile endpoint or proxy blocks the process indefinitely, which is a denial of service for a CI job. Very large responses can exhaust memory.

### Mitigation

Set connect and request timeouts, treat a timeout as a failed lookup (see failed-lookup-looks-like-clean-result) and cap the response size (see requirement bounded-network-calls). Not started."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/pagination-url-not-pinned-to-host
   :name "GitHub pagination URL is followed with the API token and no host check"
   :desc "The rel=next URL from the Link header is requested with the same headers, including the Authorization bearer token, without checking scheme or host."
   :doc "### Description

adapter.vulnerability.github-advisory/parse-link-header returns the next URL from the response and fetch-advisories requests it with the original headers. Only a response from api.github.com over TLS can supply it, and the default HttpClient does not follow redirects, so exploitation needs a compromised or impersonated endpoint. Found in the security assessment at commit 5279795; confidence medium.

### Impact

The API key could be sent to a host other than api.github.com.

### Mitigation

Accept the next URL only if its scheme is https and its host is api.github.com (see requirement credentials-only-to-own-service). Not started."}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/debug-println-to-stdout
   :name "leftover debug println writes request URLs to stdout"
   :desc "The osv, deps-dev and github-advisory adapters print 'Fetching URL: ...' to stdout, which pollutes report output and logs package names."
   :doc "### Description

adapter.vulnerability.deps-dev/fetch-json, adapter.vulnerability.github-advisory/fetch-page and adapter.vulnerability.osv/fetch-json and post-json call (println \"Fetching URL:\" url). This is a leftover debug statement, not intended behavior. Found in the security assessment at commit 5279795; confidence high. The adapters' warn helpers do write to stderr.

### Impact

Report output on stdout (edn, json, markdown) is corrupted when a live source is used, so it cannot be piped reliably. The URLs contain package names and versions, which end up in captured CI logs. It contradicts the requirement actionable-diagnostics and the decision diagnostics-on-stderr.

### Mitigation

Remove the statements, or send them to stderr behind --debug, ideally through the shared diagnostics function proposed in duplicated-warn-helpers. Not started."
   :tags #{"technical debt"}}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/global-mutable-state
   :name "application state is one global atom"
   :desc "repository/state is a single atom that adapters fill and every report function reads implicitly, which hides data flow and blocks library use."
   :doc "### Description

application.repository/state is (atom {}). Adapters write to it with swap!, and the report functions take no arguments and read it. The order of calls in adapter.ui.cli/initialize-state matters, and tests must reset the global state. Domain functions, in contrast, take their inputs explicitly. Found in the architecture assessment at commit 5279795; confidence high.

### Impact

Hidden coupling between adapters and reports. A second run in the same process (REPL, library use) sees the first run's state, and tests cannot run in parallel. The cost is low while the tool is a one-shot CLI.

### Mitigation

Return data from the adapters and let the CLI assemble an explicit state map that is passed to the report functions (changes internals only, not the public contracts). Or accept the simplification and keep this entry as its record. Open decision for the maintainer."
   :tags #{"technical debt"}}

  {:el :risk
   :id :sbom-tool.risk/duplicated-adapter-infrastructure
   :name "vulnerability adapters duplicate their HTTP infrastructure"
   :desc "osv, deps-dev and github-advisory each carry their own HTTP client, request and error handling, API-key file reader, warn and bounded-pmap."
   :doc "### Description

Beyond bounded-pmap and warn (see duplicated-bounded-pmap and duplicated-warn-helpers), the three adapters repeat the HttpClient setup, the send and error-tagging pattern, the API-key file and environment lookup and the JsonProcessingException Class/forName workaround. Found in the architecture assessment at commit 5279795; confidence high.

### Impact

Each cross-cutting fix has to be made three times, and a fourth adapter (NVD) adds a copy. This includes the open security fixes (no-network-timeouts-or-size-limits, pagination-url-not-pinned-to-host, debug-println-to-stdout).

### Mitigation

Proposed decision shared-adapter-support: one adapter-internal support namespace. Not started."
   :tags #{"technical debt"}}

